{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-326442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)=-{x}^{2}(y-2).$ Fill in the missing signs in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $y>2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3566fde9b24eb1f95424406e0207ebca91dc14d0f2400140e3162e126723c74"} {"question" "q-326442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/be201671c4862874082f9aa2bf598a19a629d808e263ac410629229bb97c8ebf.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/dc763b1ceba087be22a05c0e0f411d82e1a6ae13566f9c9625fa2e164d8be1ce.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{2}$ | $(y-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/5b2e7277212bacfb039b94783de1a669de6aed764ac8584c23f253448d4fc517.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refer