\n|  | Test point | ${x}^{2}$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/f6c800752188e6dd292d47f7153462077288540dd232f76a03472b2bab3d20c0.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3566fde9b24eb1f95424406e0207ebca91dc14d0f2400140e3162e126723c74"} {"question" "q-121092" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7411d52c7bd30ff865d94a19d5eafd0f7c62f4ed05a70bb40cc33d87ccff4fc1"} {"question" "q-121092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3566fde9b24eb1f95424406e0207ebca91dc14d0f2400140e3162e126723c74"} {"question" "q-121092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/044ec4b87a0878f35045e8e27e7998dd55bce0e3ce8123adfc8c556bfce54f83.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/275addd3a5a72dae7ef18f842684b820c977808b2abec2c1a018769cc36534bd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/91b792a89be9e6508a40371200ca62435bbb16facbf4347cad3a38cc54f13ce6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/bd697d89d5264738a6d1d2549f03a213beac6ac86811fb0d1310c88a63a7330c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/e021fa28597b7cd16ac413772f5cf17f512787861d18ea268aa6f2fd246f0f57.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3566fde9b24eb1f95424406e0207ebca91dc14d0f2400140e3162e126723c74"} {"question" "q-121092" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/91b792a89be9e6508a40371200ca62435bbb16facbf4347cad3a38cc54f13ce6.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3566fde9b24eb1f95424406e0207ebca91dc14d0f2400140e3162e126723c74"} {"question" "e-9412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<   3$ | $x> 3$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/example-9412.json" "file_sha256" "d63aa26939e8281fbf37fe33b7b5a076022312b553f0a4a9cd7ede8afa6241be"} {"question" "e-9412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/df8519853487a7da738b4b3c477b141e00287c67dcb9eb804cbd385c6f43b611.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/4666739c474f85cbf8de7e7ca183206ab7b0bd804816a5504f9c1d97b03956b4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/e789ce41c696b456e0c9d79ba8ce60aba030e454ab614902ea13c8d9ccd02d8c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<  3$ | $x> 3$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/example-9412.json" "file_sha256" "d63aa26939e8281fbf37fe33b7b5a076022312b553f0a4a9cd7ede8afa6241be"} {"question" "e-9414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y) = (x-2)^2(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) > 0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/example-9414.json" "file_sha256" "1a04175dc6f51c032ea286c75efeea59a25cdd7a3c8b2bfc1be10dd55e0bc1d4"} {"question" "e-9414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/8e635d2ba2e7ca5721ff8b1dec2055b4ac0c9bcc2eca648848c27190271e92b6.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/1ce934096086de33423910710a010400922ea86c66bbfe0b1bfb0d92291ab59e.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^2$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) > 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/70a2ca01e47e91f9d818599886f79b0f00edad0cfd12b080995dfbe6cdf68235.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/example-9414.json" "file_sha256" "1a04175dc6f51c032ea286c75efeea59a25cdd7a3c8b2bfc1be10dd55e0bc1d4"}] "usage" {"e-9412" [] "q-121090" [] "q-121092" [] "e-9414" []} "basis" 1256 "retractions" [[17592186179875 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x, y) = x(y + 1)$. Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x, y) \\ge 0$?"] [17592186179875 :question/worked-solution "The product $x(y+1)$ is nonnegative when its factors have the same sign or at least one is zero. Thus the solution is\n$$\\{x\\ge0,\\ y\\ge-1\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{x\\le0,\\ y\\le-1\\}.$$\nChoose the diagram shading the upper-right and lower-left regions relative to $x=0$ and $y=-1$, with both boundary lines included. These boundaries contain all points where the product is zero."] [17592186179875 :question/difficulty 17592186045465] [17592186179876 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x, y) = x^{2}(y + 1)$. Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x, y) < 0$?"] [17592186179876 :question/worked-solution "When $x\\ne0$, the factor $x^2$ is positive, so $x^2(y+1)<0$ precisely when $y+1<0$. At $x=0$, the product is zero and fails the strict inequality. The solution is\n$$y<-1,\\qquad x\\ne0.$$\nChoose the diagram shading below $y=-1$ on both sides of the excluded line $x=0$, with both boundaries dashed."] [17592186179493 :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2$. What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y < -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x< 3$ | $x> 3$ |"] [17592186179493 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1$. These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/b7c9a28da4eb9d34bd96f0d44ec1e023.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/59e7d4a360af087d0af49993c2fcb8b8.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/c0d3dfb30ea814e83cccbb4b403b88fa.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $y < -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x< 3$ | $x> 3$ |"] [17592186179494 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y) = (x-2)^2(y+1)$. Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) > 0$?"] [17592186179494 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1$. These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/1d27c3483fe5a41e0d1d8b6a1156162a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/e1a79695a81263b0d3e9b0ff97ee32c2.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^2$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Top-Left | $(1,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) > 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/a492e76f5893f613b3bf5fdabaebbfa9.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177251 17592186177252 17592186177253 17592186177254 17592186177255 17592186177257 17592186177258 17592186177259 17592186177260 17592186177261] "immutable_fields" [[175921861